# Symbolic differentiation as forward-chaining rules, following the
# Trigger / Decompose / Assemble / Connect architecture.
#
# Usage:
#   x ~ symvar
#   (x * x) diffby x
#   ((x * x) diffby x) = D    # repeatable result query; answers (x + x)
#
# The raw derivative is deliberately NOT the exposed result: the connect
# stage asserts an ordinary (D simplify D) request, symbolic-core
# answers it, and the simplified form is exposed under = -- the same
# cross-module cascade as MA1/MA2 in the arithmetic modules.
#
# The module functions according to NEGATION-FREE principles. Constancy
# is declared solely at the LEAVES, each via a positive rule: a
# symconst, a symvar distinct from X, or a numeral. Every compound
# expression undergoes differentiation in a structural manner,
# including a constant one -- its raw derivative is assembled from &0
# leaves and simplifies to &0. Consequently, a term acquires at most a
# single raw derivative (rule DU reports any second one as a
# contradiction), and a term that includes a subterm outside the
# vocabulary gets none: no rule applies to it.
#
# Undeclared sorts result in NO answer, never an incorrect one: each
# leaf rule is guarded by X ~ symvar and by a positive sort linked to
# the leaf, so a missing declaration results in no response. This module
# itself differentiates + and *, and an application (F of U) where F is
# ln or carries a hasderivative fact. Every other operator gets no
# derivative until a module contributes one -- partiality by absence.
#
# OPERATOR EXTENSION PROTOCOL for differentiation: an operator joins by
# contributing (1) decompose rules that relay dstate to its operands and
# (2) an assemble rule that builds its derivative from theirs. No
# additional requirements: no composite needs a constancy rule or a
# standalone shape declaration. symbolic-minus.zph contributes -, along
# with an assemble rule for neg; symbolic-pow.zph contributes ^;
# symbolic-integers.zph adds the numeral zint leaves. .import math
# imports all three.

.import symbolic-core

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Function-symbol derivatives: F hasderivative G means d F(u)/du = G(u).
# ln has a dedicated rule below -- its derivative 1/u is not of the form
# G(u) for a named symbol G.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
exp hasderivative exp

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rule DT0 -- Trigger
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T diffby X) => (T dstate X)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules DD -- Decompose
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((U + V) dstate X) => (U dstate X)
((U + V) dstate X) => (V dstate X)
((U * V) dstate X) => (U dstate X)
((U * V) dstate X) => (V dstate X)
((F of U) dstate X) => (U dstate X)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rule DN -- Numerals: a cons list arriving at dstate asks
# common-arithmetic's numeral test (rules there, "Numeral test") to
# determine if it constitutes a number within the currently loaded
# substrate. Only a numeral gets the constant leaf rule beneath; any other
# list, <x c> for example, receives no derivative.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((A cons R) dstate X) => ((A cons R) needsnumeral (A cons R))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules DB -- Base cases: the leaves. The variable alone yields derivative
# &1; every other declared leaf yields derivative &0.
#
# Observe the placement of variables: a dstate fact appears as
# (TERM dstate VARIABLE), meaning that the rules for OTHER leaves bind
# term and variable separately, and the inequality guard keeps a symbol
# from also counting as constant with respect to itself. This guard is
# significant for symconst as well: since sorts are facts, a single symbol
# can hold both (math.zph's polyring rule makes every ring indeterminate a
# symvar), and when differentiated WITH RESPECT TO such a symbol, it must
# yield &1 exclusively.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(X dstate X, X ~ symvar) => ((X wrt X) deriv &1)

(T dstate X, X ~ symvar, T ~ symvar, T != X)   => ((T wrt X) deriv &0)
(T dstate X, X ~ symvar, T ~ symconst, T != X) => ((T wrt X) deriv &0)
((A cons R) dstate X, X ~ symvar, (A cons R) isnumeral true)
=> (((A cons R) wrt X) deriv &0)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules DA -- Assemble: sum, product, chain. Consequences build the
# derivative terms as ordinary graph structure via fact().
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((U + V) dstate X,
 (U wrt X) deriv P,
 (V wrt X) deriv Q)
=> (((U + V) wrt X) deriv (P + Q))

((U * V) dstate X,
 (U wrt X) deriv P,
 (V wrt X) deriv Q)
=> (((U * V) wrt X) deriv ((P * V) + (U * Q)))

((F of U) dstate X,
 F hasderivative G,
 (U wrt X) deriv P)
=> (((F of U) wrt X) deriv ((G of U) * P))

((ln of U) dstate X,
 (U wrt X) deriv P)
=> (((ln of U) wrt X) deriv (P / U))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rule DU -- deriv is single-valued: for each term, there exists at most
# one raw derivative with respect to any given variable, because each
# shape corresponds to exactly one rule above. The appearance of a
# second derivative would indicate a flaw in the rule set -- this was
# how the constancy rule in versions up to 1.0.1 manifested: a constant
# product yielded &0 from it alongside every combination assembled by
# the product rule -- and such an outcome is reported as a contradiction
# rather than being silently propagated.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((T wrt X) deriv A, (T wrt X) deriv B, A != B) => !

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules DC -- Connect: pipe the raw derivative through the simplifier
# (cross-module cascade), then expose the simplified form under =.
# Repeatable query: (T diffby X) = D
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T diffby X, (T wrt X) deriv D) => (D simplify D)

(T diffby X,
 (T wrt X) deriv D,
 (D simplify D) = S)
=> ((T diffby X) = S)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules DL -- Iterated differentiation along a cons list of variables.
#
#   (T diffalong <x y>) = E     d/dy (d/dx T)  -- reading order, outermost
#                               cell first, as in <x y z> ~ polyring
#   (T diffalong <x x>) = E     the second derivative
#   (T diffalong nil)   = T     the empty list differentiates nothing
#
# No new machinery: each step is an ordinary diffby request whose = result
# feeds the next one -- results are inputs again, the property the whole
# stdlib is built on. A cons list is used rather than a fresh pair former
# because facts carry a SET of objects, so (T d X Y) could not distinguish
# (T d x y) from (T d y x); the list fixes the order.
#
# Partiality composes: if any step derives no result, the chain stays
# silent. When mixed partials answer in both orders, their values align --
# one order might remain silent while the other provides a response, due
# to the derivative of ln being a quotient -- and whenever the two
# simplified expressions match, they converge on the identical answer node
# -- the Schwarz/Clairaut symmetry, revealed through node identity rather
# than presupposed, as demonstrated by ((x * x) * y). The simplifier does
# not enforce normalization of commutativity or associativity, hence the
# two orders may conclude with distinct configurations of the same value:
# ((x * y) * (x + y)) gives (((x + y) + y) + x) along <x y> and
# (((x + y) + x) + y) along <y x>.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T diffalong nil) => ((T diffalong nil) = T)

(T diffalong (V cons R)) => (T diffby V)

(T diffalong (V cons R), (T diffby V) = D) => (D diffalong R)

(T diffalong (V cons R),
 (T diffby V) = D,
 (D diffalong R) = E)
=> ((T diffalong (V cons R)) = E)
